arXiv:2510.21542v1 [stat.ML] 24 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Johann Flemming Gloy
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science and EngineeringChalmers University of Technology and University of GothenburgSE-41296 Gothenburg, Sweden.
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gloy@chalmers.se</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Simon Olsson
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science and EngineeringChalmers University of Technology and University of GothenburgSE-41296 Gothenburg, Sweden.
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">simonols@chalmers.se</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow and diffusion-based models have emerged as powerful tools for scientific applications, particularly for sampling non-normalized probability distributions, as exemplified by Boltzmann Generators (BGs). A critical challenge in deploying these models is their reliance on sample likelihood computations, which scale prohibitively with system size <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, often rendering them infeasible for large-scale problems. To address this, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HollowFlow</span>, a flow-based generative model leveraging a novel non-backtracking graph neural network (NoBGNN). By enforcing a block-diagonal Jacobian structure, HollowFlow likelihoods are evaluated with a constant number of backward passes in <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding speed-ups of up to <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>: a significant step towards scaling BGs to larger systems. Crucially, our framework generalizes: <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">any equivariant GNN or attention-based architecture</span> can be adapted into a NoBGNN. We validate HollowFlow by training BGs on two different systems of increasing size. For both systems, the sampling and likelihood evaluation time decreases dramatically, following our theoretical scaling laws. For the larger system we obtain a <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10^{2}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{2}\times</annotation></semantics></math> speed-up, clearly illustrating the potential of HollowFlow-based approaches for high-dimensional scientific problems previously hindered by computational bottlenecks.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/overview.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/192;" width="405" height="192"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of HollowFlow<span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: (a) A vector field <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is parametrized with an (equivariant) non-backtracking graph neural network (NoBGNN) and a series of (equivariant) neural networks <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that its Jacobian <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> (b) can be decomposed into a block-hollow and block-diagonal part with block size <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (in this example, <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3,n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3,n=5</annotation></semantics></math>). (c) Efficient evaluation of the trace of the Jacobian with only <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes (BP) through the network. The vector components are one where the entry is green and zero elsewhere.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Efficiently sampling high-dimensional, non-normalized densities — a cornerstone of Bayesian inference and molecular dynamics — remains computationally intractable for many scientific applications. Boltzmann generators (BGs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> address this by training a surrogate model <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the Boltzmann distribution <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathbf{x})=Z^{-1}\exp(-\beta u(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathbf{x})=Z^{-1}\exp(-\beta u(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the potential energy of the system configuration <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> the inverse temperature, and <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> the intractable partition function. While BGs enable unbiased estimation of observables through reweighting <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\sim\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\sim\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with weights <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\propto\mu(\mathbf{x}_{i})/\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\propto\mu(\mathbf{x}_{i})/\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, their practicality is limited by a fundamental trade-off: likelihood computations of expressive surrogates rely on <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> automatic differentiation backward passes with system dimensionality <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, rendering them infeasible for large-scale problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this problem, we outline a framework combining non-backtracking equivariant graph neural networks with continuous normalizing flows (CNFs), breaking this trade-off. This strategy enforces a Jacobian that can easily be split into a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">block-diagonal</span> and a <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">block-hollow</span> part, reducing the number of backward passes required for likelihood computation to scale as <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Our contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hollow Message Passing (HoMP):</span> A general scheme for message passing with a block-diagonal Jacobian structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Proofs and Guarantees:</span> A proof of the block-diagonal structure of HoMP and scaling laws of the computational complexity of forward and backward passes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalable Boltzmann Generators:</span> A HollowFlow-based BG implementation achieving <math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10^{2}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{2}\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster sampling and likelihood evaluation, e.g., <math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10^{2}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{2}\times</annotation></semantics></math> for LJ55, a system of 55 particles in 3-dimensions interacting via pair-wise Lennard-Jones potentials.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Experiments on multi-particle systems validate HollowFlow’s scalability, bridging a critical gap in high-dimensional generative modelling for the sciences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Boltzmann Generators and Observables </h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Computing observables by averaging over the Boltzmann distribution is a central challenge in chemistry, physics, and biology. The Boltzmann distribution is given by,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathbf{x})=Z^{-1}\exp(-\beta u(\mathbf{x}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathbf{x})=Z^{-1}\exp(-\beta u(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=(k_{\textrm{B}}T)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>B</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=(k_{\textrm{B}}T)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse thermal energy and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Omega\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Omega\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the potential energy of a system configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Observables are experimentally measurable quantities, such as free energies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Generating ensembles which align with certain observables can give us fundamental insights into the physics of molecules, including mechanisms, rates, and affinities of drugs binding their targets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, protein folding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, or phase transitions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The primary strategies for generating samples from the Boltzmann distribution involve Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) or molecular dynamics (MD) simulations. These approaches generate unbiased samples from the Boltzmann distribution asymptotically, however, mix slowly on the complex free energy landscape of physical systems, where multiple, metastable states are separated by low-probability channels. In turn, extremely long simulations are needed to generate independent sampling statistics using these methods. In the molecular simulation community, this problem has stimulated the development of a host of enhanced sampling methods, for a recent survey, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Boltzmann Generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> instead try to learn an efficient surrogate from biased simulation data, and recover unbiased sampling statistics through importance weighting. The key advantage of BGs is that sampling using modern generative methods, such as normalizing flows, is much faster than the conventional simulation based approaches, which would enable amortization of the compute cost per Boltzmann distributed sample.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Normalizing Flows</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable reweighting, evaluating the sample likelihood under a model, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, is critical. A <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normalizing flow</span> (NF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> allows efficient sampling, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> sample likelihood evaluation, by parameterizing a diffeomorphism, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a constrained Jacobian structure. The diffeomorphism (or <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flow</span>) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> maps an easy-to-sample base distribution, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to an approximation <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the empirical data density, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., by minimizing the forward Kullback-Leibler divergence, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{D}||\rho_{1})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{D}}\left[\log p_{D}(\mathbf{x})-\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{D}||\rho_{1})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim p_{D}}\left[\log p_{D}(\mathbf{x})-\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. The change in log-density when transforming samples with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\log\rho^{\text{NF}}(\mathbf{x}):=\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})-\log\rho_{0}(\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x}))=\log\left|\det\frac{\partial\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x})}{\partial\mathbf{x}}\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>NF</mtext></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\log\rho^{\text{NF}}(\mathbf{x}):=\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x})-\log\rho_{0}(\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x}))=\log\left|\det\frac{\partial\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x})}{\partial\mathbf{x}}\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Computing the log-determinant of a general <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> Jacobian is computationally expensive (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(N^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(N^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>), however, endowing the Jacobian <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x})}{\partial\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\phi_{\theta}^{-1}(\mathbf{x})}{\partial\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with structure, i.e., triangular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, can make the likelihood evaluation fast.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Continuous Normalizing Flows</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A flow can also be parametrized as the solution to an initial value problem, specified by a velocity field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t):\mathbb{R}^{N}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t):\mathbb{R}^{N}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of an ordinary differential equation (ODE)</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}(t)=b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)\,\mathrm{d}t,\,\,\mathbf{x}(0)\sim\rho_{0},\,\,\mathbf{x}(1)\sim\rho_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}(t)=b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)\,\mathrm{d}t,\,\,\mathbf{x}(0)\sim\rho_{0},\,\,\mathbf{x}(1)\sim\rho_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The general solution to this problem is a flow, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t):\mathbb{R}^{N}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t):\mathbb{R}^{N}\times[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfies the initial condition, e.g., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(0),0)=\mathbf{x}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(0),0)=\mathbf{x}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a model is known as a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous normalizing flow </span>(CNF). The ODE <a href="#S2.E3" title="In Continuous Normalizing Flows ‣ 2.2 Normalizing Flows ‣ 2 Background ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> together with the prior <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a time-dependent distribution <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mathbf{x}(t),t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathbf{x}(t),t)</annotation></semantics></math> given by the continuity equation</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\partial_{t}\rho_{t}=-\nabla\cdot(\rho_{t}b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\partial_{t}\rho_{t}=-\nabla\cdot(\rho_{t}b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⋅</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the partial derivative w.r.t. <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the divergence operator, respectively. Solving <a href="#S2.E4" title="In Continuous Normalizing Flows ‣ 2.2 Normalizing Flows ‣ 2 Background ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we get the change in log-probability for the CNF,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\log\rho^{\text{CNF}}:=\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x}(1))-\log\rho_{0}(\mathbf{x}(0))=-\int_{0}^{1}\nabla\cdot b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)\,\mathrm{d}t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>log</mi><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>CNF</mtext></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\log\rho^{\text{CNF}}:=\log\rho_{1}(\mathbf{x}(1))-\log\rho_{0}(\mathbf{x}(0))=-\int_{0}^{1}\nabla\cdot b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)\,\mathrm{d}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In principle, CNFs allow for much more expressive architectures to be used to parametrize <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The currently most scalable approach to train CNFs is conditional flow matching (CFM) or related objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. In CFM, the intractable flow matching loss, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is regressed directly against the unknown true vector field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, of a flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, is replaced by the conditional flow matching loss which has the same gradient w.r.t. the parameters <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{x}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{z}\sim p(\mathbf{z})}||b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t)-u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})||_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CFM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1],\mathbf{x}\sim p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z}),\mathbf{z}\sim p(\mathbf{z})}||b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},t)-u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})||_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The true vector field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding probability density <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are given as marginals over the conditional probability and an arbitrary conditioning distribution <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|z)p(\mathbf{z})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}\quad\text{and}\quad u_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int\frac{p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})}{p_{t}(\mathbf{x})}p(\mathbf{z})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|z)p(\mathbf{z})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}\quad\text{and}\quad u_{t}(\mathbf{x})=\int\frac{p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})}{p_{t}(\mathbf{x})}p(\mathbf{z})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where we parameterize <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> with a linear interpolant with connections to optimal transport theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1}),\quad p(\mathbf{z})=\pi(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\quad p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})=\mathcal{N}(t\mathbf{x}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma),\quad u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})=\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1}),\quad p(\mathbf{z})=\pi(\mathbf{x}_{0},\mathbf{x}_{1})\quad p_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})=\mathcal{N}(t\mathbf{x}_{1}+(1-t)\mathbf{x}_{0},\sigma),\quad u_{t}(\mathbf{x}|\mathbf{z})=\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the coupling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the 2-Wasserstein optimal transport map between the prior <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}(\mathbf{x}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and the target <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathbf{x}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathbf{x}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> using a mini-batch approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, akin to general Jacobian log-determinant calculation of normalizing flows, computation of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\cdot b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\cdot b_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}(t),t)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Continuous Normalizing Flows ‣ 2.2 Normalizing Flows ‣ 2 Background ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) requires <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes, for each numerical integration step along <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, making the evaluation of sample likelihoods impractical in high-dimensions, and consequently, their use for BGs limited.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equivariant flows</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Symmetries can be described in terms of a group <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts on a finite-dimensional vector space <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\cong\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\cong\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> through a group representation <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathcal{G}\rightarrow GL(d,\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathcal{G}\rightarrow GL(d,\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. A function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\rho(g)x)=\rho(g)f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\rho(g)x)=\rho(g)f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\rho(g)\mathbf{x})=h(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\rho(g)\mathbf{x})=h(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is another vector space. If the vector field <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> of a CNF is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}&lt;\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}&lt;\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the prior density <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant it has been shown that the push forward density, of the flow <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. In practical applications, symmetries under the Euclidean group <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(3)</annotation></semantics></math> are of particular interest as these are the symmetries that are naturally obeyed by many physical systems such as molecules. Thus, several GNN architectures have been proposed that are equivariant or invariant under the action of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(3)</annotation></semantics></math> or some subgroup of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(3)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>HollowNets</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reverse-mode automatic differentiation, or back-propagation, allows for the computation of vector-Jacobian products with a cost which is asymptotically equal to that of the forward pass <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. In practice, we use this mode to compute the divergence of velocity fields parametrized with neural networks. However, isolating the diagonal of the Jacobian to compute the divergence, for general, free-form vector fields, requires <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using a special neural network construction <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HollowNet</span>, Chen and Duvenaud showed, that one can access the full diagonal of the Jacobian, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a single backward pass.
A HollowNet depends on two components:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{Conditioner: }h_{i}=c_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{\neg i}),\text{where }c_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{N-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Conditioner: </mtext><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>where </mtext><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{Conditioner: }h_{i}=c_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{\neg i}),\text{where }c_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{N-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{Transformer: }b_{i}=\tau_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{h}_{i}),\text{where }\tau_{i}:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Transformer: </mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>where </mtext><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{Transformer: }b_{i}=\tau_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{h}_{i}),\text{where }\tau_{i}:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a latent embedding which is independent of the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’th dimension of the input, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the chain rule to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=\tau_{i}(r_{i},c_{i}(r_{\neg i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=\tau_{i}(r_{i},c_{i}(r_{\neg i}))</annotation></semantics></math>, the Jacobian <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}=\frac{\mathrm{d}b}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐉</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}=\frac{\mathrm{d}b}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}b}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}=\underbrace{\frac{\partial\tau}{\partial\mathbf{x}}}_{\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}}+\underbrace{\frac{\partial\tau}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}}{\partial\mathbf{x}}}_{\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{hollow}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>diag</mi></msup></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>hollow</mi></msup></munder></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}b}{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}}=\underbrace{\frac{\partial\tau}{\partial\mathbf{x}}}_{\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}}+\underbrace{\frac{\partial\tau}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}}{\partial\mathbf{x}}}_{\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{hollow}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which splits the Jacobian into diagonal <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>diag</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math> and ‘hollow’ <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{hollow}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>hollow</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{hollow}}</annotation></semantics></math> contributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. The divergence can then be evaluated in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes, through rewiring the compute graph and computing <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{1}^{T}\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">𝟙</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>diag</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{1}^{T}\mathbf{J}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hollow Message Passing</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is straightforward to construct HollowNets for regular feed-forward neural networks, and convolutional neural networks, through masking operations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. However, extending these principles to cases where the data or its domain are subject to other symmetries is not as straightforward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this problem, we develop <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hollow Message Passing</span> (HoMP) to work with graph data, subject to permutation equivariance. We further specialize this approach to work with data which is subject to common Euclidean symmetries, useful in molecular applications. Here, molecules and particle systems are represented as points in <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (typically <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>), one for each of the <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> atoms or particles, making <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=dn</annotation></semantics></math>. HoMP depends on the following developments:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Permutation Equivariant Conditioner:</span> We make the conditioner functions <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> permutation equivariant, using a <span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">non-backtracking GNN</span> (NoBGNN) in combination with a changing underlying graph structure such that it fulfils the same constraints as the original conditioner. Namely, <math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial h_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{i}}=0\ \forall i\in\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial h_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{i}}=0\ \forall i\in\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features:</span> We lift the HollowNet from working with scalar features, i.e., <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to work with Euclidean features, e.g., <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, NoBGNN allows for the use of message passing architectures that are invariant or equivariant under the group action <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g),g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g),g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of a group <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, for each <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> there can be additional scalar input data <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice (see <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinates of an atom while <math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the atom type.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Permutation Equivariant Conditioner</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a GNN for the conditioner such that information from any node never returns to itself. A GNN where this holds for one message passing step is called a non-backtracking GNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> or a line graph GNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and can be constructed as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(N,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(N,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a directed graph with nodes <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and edges <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E\subseteq\{(i,j)|i,j\in N,i\neq j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq\{(i,j)|i,j\in N,i\neq j\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To each node <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign a node feature <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct the non-backtracking line graph <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)=(N^{lg},E^{lg})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)=(N^{lg},E^{lg})</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle N^{lg}=E,\quad E^{lg}=\{(i,j,k)|(i,j)\in E,(j,k)\in E\text{ and }i\neq k\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N^{lg}=E,\quad E^{lg}=\{(i,j,k)|(i,j)\in E,(j,k)\in E\text{ and }i\neq k\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The node features of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{lg}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{lg}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined to be</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n^{lg}_{ij}=n_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n^{lg}_{ij}=n_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The hidden states <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized to be <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}_{ij}=n^{lg}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}_{ij}=n^{lg}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this setup, a standard message passing scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> is applied on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m^{t}_{lij}=\phi(h^{t}_{li},h^{t}_{ij}),\quad m^{t}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m^{t}_{lij}=\phi(h^{t}_{li},h^{t}_{ij}),\quad m^{t}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)}m^{t}_{lij},\quad h^{t+1}_{ij}=\psi(h^{t}_{ij},m^{t}_{ij})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)}m^{t}_{lij},\quad h^{t+1}_{ij}=\psi(h^{t}_{ij},m^{t}_{ij}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> are all learnable functions, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)=\{k|(k,i,j)\in E^{lg}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)=\{k|(k,i,j)\in E^{lg}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(i)=\{k|(k,i)\in E\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(i)=\{k|(k,i)\in E\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of message passing steps on the line graph.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/LG_a.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/LG_b.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/LG_c.svg" id="S3.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Schematic example of line graph message passing.</span> Blue: original graph (<math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>), orange: line graph (<math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>), gray: information flow between <math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> Construction of the line graph nodes. Every node in <math id="S3.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to one edge in G. The dashed arrows indicate information flow before the message passing from <math id="S3.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="Figure 2(b) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> Construction of the edges of <math id="S3.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S3.F2.sf3" title="Figure 2(c) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(c)</span></a> Information flow after the message passing (dashed arrows) from <math id="S3.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This construction is illustrated in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The blue nodes and edges are part of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> while the orange nodes and edges are part of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. The gray dashed arrow in <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> illustrate the projection of the node features onto <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). The orange arrows in <a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> illustrate the message passing on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). <a href="#S3.F2.sf3" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(c)</span></a> visualizes the projection of the node features from <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> back to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as summarized in <a href="#S3.E15" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. <a href="#S3.E13" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> take the role of the conditioner <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> while <a href="#S3.E15" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> takes the role of the transformer <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(h,\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(h,\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">So far, this GNN is, except in special cases that we detail in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, only non-backtracking if just one message passing step is performed. In general, to ensure the non-backtracking nature of the GNN, some connections in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> need to be removed after an appropriate number of message passing steps. This can be done by keeping track of which nodes of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> received information of which nodes of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> after each message passing step. To this end, we define the three dimensional time-dependent backtracking array <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B(t)_{ijk}=\begin{cases}1\quad\text{if}\ \frac{\partial h^{t}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}\neq 0\\
0\quad\text{else.}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>else.</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B(t)_{ijk}=\begin{cases}1\quad\text{if}\ \frac{\partial h^{t}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}\neq 0\\
0\quad\text{else.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with the additional convention that <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial h^{t}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial h^{t}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\notin E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\notin E</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that whenever <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)_{ijj}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)_{ijj}=1</annotation></semantics></math> information that started in node <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> will return to node <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> during readout. <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows in detail how <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated (steps 8 and 11) and used to remove edges in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (step 10). A more detailed explanation and a proof that this construction does indeed result in a non-backtracking GNN for an arbitrary number of message passing steps is given in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features:</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In typical molecular applications, nodes are represented in Euclidean space (e.g., <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>) and may have additional features <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (such as atom type). Our goal is to embed <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> into a set of equivariant (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and invariant (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) node features, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=(v_{i},s_{i})=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=(v_{i},s_{i})=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}\times d}\times\mathbb{R}^{n_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> which serve as inputs for message passing on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>(see <a href="#S3.E13" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>). For this task, we can use any <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant embeddings inherent to GNN architectures such as PaiNN or E3NN (for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}&lt;E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}&lt;E(3)</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The message passing and readout functions (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) are made <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant by using the message and update functions of any <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant GNN. As we only modify the underlying graph of the GNN, this trivially ensures equivariance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By construction, the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional node inputs <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> allow the Jacobian <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> to be easily split into a block-diagonal and block-hollow part with block size <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation and its consequences are summarized in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Block-hollowness of HoMP). ‣ Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for a proof and additional explanations). <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can also run on the pairwise euclidean differences of the node features <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pd}=true" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>pd</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pd}=true</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) . This is common practice in molecular applications to achieve translation invariance.
A brief illustration of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be found in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Hollow message passing</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\text{ where }\ \mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ and }Z_{i}\in\mathbb{R},\quad\text{pd}\in\{true,false\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>pd</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\text{ where }\ \mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ and }Z_{i}\in\mathbb{R},\quad\text{pd}\in\{true,false\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n},\ b_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n},\ b_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
