<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{t}_{kij}=\phi(h^{t}_{ij},h^{t}_{ki}),\quad m^{t}_{ij}=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)}m^{t}_{kij},\quad h^{t+1}_{ij}=\psi(h^{t}_{ij},m^{t}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{t}_{kij}=\phi(h^{t}_{ij},h^{t}_{ki}),\quad m^{t}_{ij}=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)}m^{t}_{kij},\quad h^{t+1}_{ij}=\psi(h^{t}_{ij},m^{t}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
              
              
            Perform readout and project back to <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>:

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{ij}),\quad\text{where }n_{ij}=\begin{cases}\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i}.\texttt{detach()}-\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{i})\quad\text{if }\text{pd}\\
n_{j}\quad\text{else}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embed</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">detach()</mtext><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>pd</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>else</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{ij}),\quad\text{where }n_{ij}=\begin{cases}\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i}.\texttt{detach()}-\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{i})\quad\text{if }\text{pd}\\
n_{j}\quad\text{else}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Block-hollowness of HoMP)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defines a function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:\mathbb{R}^{dn}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:\mathbb{R}^{dn}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{dn}</annotation></semantics></math> whose Jacobian <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn\times dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn\times dn}</annotation></semantics></math> can be split into a block-hollow and a block-diagonal part, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}=\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}+\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}=\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}+\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math> is block-hollow, while <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math> is block-diagonal with block size <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This structure of the Jacobian enables the exact computation of differential operators that only include diagonal terms of the Jacobian with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes through the network.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Adaptation of HollowFlow to Attention ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we show how HoMP can be adapted to attention mechanisms as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Computational Complexity</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Graph Connectivity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of backward passes through a vector field <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> parametrized by <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that is required to compute its divergence is reduced by a factor of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> compared to a standard GNN. However, the forward pass through <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is slower than through a standard GNN, as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is operating on the line graph. This overhead will scale with the number of edges, which we denote <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Graph Connectivity ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we show how the number of edges varies with graph types. For a fully connected graph, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> scales as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> resulting in an impractical overhead in high-dimensional systems. To address this, we consider <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nearest-neighbors (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN) graphs instead which scale as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E^{lg}=\mathcal{O}(nk^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E^{lg}=\mathcal{O}(nk^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Choosing <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math> will leave the cost of a forward-pass through <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> comparable to a standard GNN with a fully connected graph. To test whether this restriction in connectivity results in a sufficiently expressive model, we empirically test different values for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph poses a locality assumption that might limit the model’s ability to learn the equilibrium distribution of systems with long-range interaction (e.g., systems with coulomb interactions such as molecules). One possible strategy to circumvent this problem is described in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Number of edges of <math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">fully connected</th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors</th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nk)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(nk^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(nk^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Divergence Calculation Speed-up</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our runtime estimations for one integration step during inference <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{step}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{step}</annotation></semantics></math>, including likelihood calculations, for HollowFlow and a standard GNN based flow model in the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a GNN-based flow model with a fully connected graph <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{fc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{fc}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> message passing steps and a HollowFlow model with a <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors graph <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> message passing steps. Let both graphs have <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional node features.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The computational complexity of sampling from the fully connected GNN-based flow model including sample likelihoods is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">while the complexity of the HollowFlow model for the same task is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, the speed-up of HollowFlow compared to the GNN-based flow model is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A derivation of these estimates can be found in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Computational Complexities ‣ Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test HollowFlow on two Lennard-Jones systems with 13 and 55 particles, respectively, (LJ13, LJ55). We also present results on Alanine dipeptide (ALA2) in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Alanine Dipeptide ‣ Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we train a CNF (for training details and hyperparameters see <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>) using the conditional flow matching loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to sample from the equilibrium distribution of these systems. We parametrize the vector field of our CNF using the <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant PaiNN architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. We additionally use minibatch optimal transport <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and equivariant optimal transport <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. For each system we train multiple different models:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline:</span> <math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant GNN on a fully connected graph with 3–7 message passing steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HollowFlow</span>: <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant HollowFlow on a <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph with different values for <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with two message passing steps.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of LJ13, we systematically test all values of <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> in steps of <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to gain insight into how <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> affects the performance and runtime of the model. Based on our findings and the theoretical scaling laws, we pick <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{7,27\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{7,27\}</annotation></semantics></math> for LJ55.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the symmetry of pairwise interactions between atoms, all <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graphs are symmetrized, i.e., whenever the edge <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is included in the <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph the edge <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,i)</annotation></semantics></math> is included as well, even if it is not naturally part of the <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">All training details and hyperparameters are reported in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Criteria to Assess Effectiveness</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure the sampling efficiency through the Kish effective samples size (ESS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. As the ESS is highly sensitive to outliers, we remove a right and left percentile of the log importance weights, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, to get a more robust, yet biased, metric, ESS<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> (see <a href="#A4.SS7" title="D.7 Weight Clipping ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. As HoMP is, in general, a restriction compared to normal message passing, we expect the ESS to be lower. However, as long as the speed-up is sufficiently large, our method can still be an overall improvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To gauge the improvement we introduce a compute normalized, relative ESS, i.e., the number of effective samples per GPU second and GPU usage during inference (see <a href="#S5.E21" title="In Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). This gives us fairer grounds for comparison of HollowFlow against our baseline, a standard <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant CNF. Further, by dividing the relative ESS for all hollow architectures by the baseline relative ESS, we get the effective speed-up (EffSU) and correspondingly the EffSU<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> calculated from the relative ESS<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> in the same way. To ensure a fair comparison, we ran all inference tasks on the same type of GPU for every system, respectively. Details can be found in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results for LJ13 and LJ55 are listed in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. For both systems we observe that the ESS and the ESS<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> are highest for the baseline and significantly smaller for HollowFlow. However, the relative ESS<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> is significantly higher for all HollowFlow models. This is reflected in the effective speed-up EffSU<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub>, gauging how many times more samples we can generate per compute compared to our non-hollow baseline. While the maximal EffSU<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> that we observe for LJ13 is about <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, for LJ55 we observe a maximal EffSU<sub id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rem</span></sub> of about <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="93.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>93.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">93.7</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that HollowFlow can reduce the compute required for sampling by about two orders of magnitude on our larger test system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we provide a detailed analysis of the runtime of different parts of models trained on LJ13 during sampling. <a href="#S5.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> demonstrates that the vast majority of the compute in the baseline model is spent on the divergence calculations while only a tiny fraction is spent on the forward pass. <a href="#S5.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> shows that exactly the opposite is true for HollowFlow, resulting in a decrease of runtime by a factor of up to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 15</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ relative ESS}=\frac{\text{ESS}\times\text{\#samples}}{\text{RT}\times\text{(GPU usage)}},\quad\text{EffSU}(\cdot)=\frac{\text{ relative ESS}(\cdot)}{{\text{relative ESS}}(\text{baseline})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>relative ESS</mtext><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mtext>ESS</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>#samples</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>(GPU usage)</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>EffSU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mtext> relative ESS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>relative ESS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>baseline</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ relative ESS}=\frac{\text{ESS}\times\text{\#samples}}{\text{RT}\times\text{(GPU usage)}},\quad\text{EffSU}(\cdot)=\frac{\text{ relative ESS}(\cdot)}{{\text{relative ESS}}(\text{baseline})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/speedup.svg" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/308;" width="476" height="308"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21542v1/runtime.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/308;" width="476" height="308"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S5.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtimes of HollowFlow and baseline for generating <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> LJ13 equilibrium samples.</span> All runtimes are multiplied by the GPU usage. <a href="#S5.F3.sf1" title="Figure 3(a) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> Total runtime <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math> and runtime of the forward passes <math id="S5.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{f}(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{f}(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math> and backward passes <math id="S5.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math> of HollowFlow. In light blue: speed-up (ratio of the runtimes of the fully connected baseline <math id="S5.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>) and HollowFlow . <a href="#S5.F3.sf2" title="Figure 3(b) ‣ Figure 3 ‣ Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> Total runtime and runtime of the forward and backward passes for the non-hollow fully connected baseline. The forward and backward runtimes of the baseline were extrapolated from other runs, see <a href="#A4.SS6" title="D.6 Runtimes ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.6</span></a> While the total runtime of the baseline is massively dominated by the backward pass, the opposite is true for HollowFlow.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of HollowFlow trained using different <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graphs and the non-hollow fully connected baseline, all trained on LJ13. The two rightmost columns show the effective speed-up compared to the baseline <a href="#S5.E21" title="In Criteria to Assess Effectiveness ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> with and without weight clipping. Bold indicates best performance.</span></figcaption>
