</figure>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Block-hollowness of HoMP). ‣ Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Block-hollowness of HoMP)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defines a function <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:\mathbb{R}^{dn}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:\mathbb{R}^{dn}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{dn}</annotation></semantics></math> whose Jacobian <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn\times dn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn\times dn}</annotation></semantics></math> can be split into a block-hollow and a block-diagonal part, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}=\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}+\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{J}_{b(\mathbf{x})}=\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}+\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{c(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math> is block-hollow, while <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{\tau(\mathbf{x})}</annotation></semantics></math> is block-diagonal with block size <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This structure of the Jacobian enables the exact computation of differential operators that only include diagonal terms of the Jacobian with <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes through the network.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark:</span> <a href="#A2.F4" title="In Notational remarks ‣ B.1 Hollowness of HollowFlow ‣ Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> might be a helpful illustration to keep track of the indices when reading the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the chain rule step 14 gives</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{db_{j}}{d\mathbf{x}_{k}}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}\left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}}\frac{\partial h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}+\frac{\partial R}{\partial n_{ij}}\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{db_{j}}{d\mathbf{x}_{k}}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}\left(\frac{\partial R}{\partial h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}}\frac{\partial h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}+\frac{\partial R}{\partial n_{ij}}\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">If we show that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ \forall t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ \forall t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> and for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> the Jacobian decomposes as stated. We split the proof into two cases:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1: <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pd}=false" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>pd</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pd}=false</annotation></semantics></math>:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">That <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We show <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ </annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> by induction on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base case:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i},Z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is by definition independent of <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0)_{ijk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0)_{ijk}</annotation></semantics></math> is by definition one exactly when <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and zero otherwise (see step 8).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Induction step:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume</p>
<ol id="A2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)_{ijk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)_{ijk}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero exactly when <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We want to show that these conditions also hold for <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To see that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t+1}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t+1}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>, by step 12 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the first induction hypothesis, we only need to show that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ki}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ki}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ki}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ki}</annotation></semantics></math> does depend on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the second induction hypothesis, this implies <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)_{kij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)_{kij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and by step 10 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> thus <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,i,j)\notin E^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,i,j)\notin E^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we prove (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t+1)_{ijk}=0\text{ if and only if }h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if and only if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is independent of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t+1)_{ijk}=0\text{ if and only if }h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) by proving both implications:
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t+1)_{ijk}=0\implies h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is independent of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t+1)_{ijk}=0\implies h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<br class="ltx_break">Assume <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{ijk}(t+1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{ijk}(t+1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By step 11 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> this implies <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)_{lik}=0\ \forall\ l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">∀</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)_{lik}=0\ \forall\ l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)_{ijk}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)_{ijk}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By the second induction hypothesis it follows that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{li}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{li}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> which by step 12 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implies that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t+1}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t+1}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}\implies B(t+1)_{ijk}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is independent of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t+1}_{ij}\text{ is independent of }\mathbf{x}_{k}\implies B(t+1)_{ijk}=0</annotation></semantics></math>:
<br class="ltx_break">Assume <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t+1}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t+1}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. By step 12 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> this implies that <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{li}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{li}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the second induction hypothesis and step 11 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we get <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t+1)_{ijk}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t+1)_{ijk}=0</annotation></semantics></math> as desired.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2: <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{pd}=true" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>pd</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{pd}=true</annotation></semantics></math>:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">That <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial n_{ij}}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{k}}=0\text{ if }j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i}.\texttt{detach()}-\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">embed</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">detach()</mtext><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}=\texttt{embed}(\mathbf{x}_{i}.\texttt{detach()}-\mathbf{x}_{j},Z_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{detach}()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">detach</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{detach}()</annotation></semantics></math> method means that we ignore derivatives w.r.t. the detached variable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We again show <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{x}_{j}}h^{t}_{ij}=0\ </annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and for all <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> by induction on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The only difference compared to case 1 is the base case, as <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized differently.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base case:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{lg}_{ki}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{lg}_{ki}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We need to show <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0)_{ijk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0)_{ijk}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero exactly when <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0)_{ijk}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0)_{ijk}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> by step 8. Thus, by definition of <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (step 8), <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, let <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{ij}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{ij}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be independent of <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\notin\mathcal{N}^{lg}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> meaning <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0)_{ijk}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0)_{ijk}=0</annotation></semantics></math> by step 8.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Induction step:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The induction step is the same as in case 1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Jacobian diagonal with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes:</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can now exploit the decomposition <a href="#A2.E31" title="In Proof. ‣ Notational remarks ‣ B.1 Hollowness of HollowFlow ‣ Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> of the Jacobian to get all its diagonal terms with only <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> vector-Jacobian products (backward passes). This can be achieved by applying the <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">detach()</span> operation to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> in step 14 of <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> such that the block-diagonal part of the decomposition <a href="#A2.E31" title="In Proof. ‣ Notational remarks ‣ B.1 Hollowness of HollowFlow ‣ Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> remains unchanged while the hollow part vanishes. If we construct <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> column vectors <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}\}_{i=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{dn}\}_{i=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A2.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(v_{i})_{j}=\begin{cases}1\quad\text{if}\ j=i+d(k-1),\ k\in\{1,...,n\}\\
0\quad\text{else,}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>else,</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(v_{i})_{j}=\begin{cases}1\quad\text{if}\ j=i+d(k-1),\ k\in\{1,...,n\}\\
0\quad\text{else,}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The vector-Jacobian products <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{J}_{b}v_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{J}_{b}v_{i}\}_{i=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
do then give us access to all diagonal terms of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{tr}(\mathbf{J}_{b})=\sum_{i=1}^{d}v_{i}^{T}\mathbf{J}_{b}v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{tr}(\mathbf{J}_{b})=\sum_{i=1}^{d}v_{i}^{T}\mathbf{J}_{b}v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an illustration in the case <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5,d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5,d=3</annotation></semantics></math>).
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Further remarks</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conditioner <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and the transformer <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(h,\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(h,\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> shown in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can explicitly be written component-wise as follows:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{j}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{j}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}\}_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}:=h_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}\}_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}:=h_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau_{j}(h_{j},\mathbf{x}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau_{j}(h_{j},\mathbf{x}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{ij})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)}R(h_{ij}^{T^{lg}},n_{ij}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{T^{lg}}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Whenever a directed graph <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acyclic, its directed line graph <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> will also be acyclic which implies that even without edge removal in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (step 10 in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) message passing on the line graph ensures that information starting from node <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can never return to itself after an arbitrary number of message passing steps. If the shortest cycle in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has length <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">girth</span> <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>), one can do at most <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-2</annotation></semantics></math> message passing steps without returning information if no edges of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are removed. The shift <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the fact that initialization of the node features (step 7 in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and the readout (step 14 in <a href="#alg1" title="In Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) effectively bridge one edge of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px9.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> each.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Computational Complexities</h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem3a" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3a.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S4.EGx16" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Divergence Calculation Speed-up ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span><span id="Thmtheorem3a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a GNN-based flow model with a fully connected graph <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{fc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{fc}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> message passing steps and a HollowFlow model with a <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors graph <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> message passing steps. Let both graphs have <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and <math id="Thmtheorem3a.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional node features.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3a.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The computational complexity of sampling from the fully connected GNN-based flow model including sample likelihoods is</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S4.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Divergence Calculation Speed-up ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">while the complexity of the HollowFlow model for the same task is</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S4.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Divergence Calculation Speed-up ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3a.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, the speed-up of HollowFlow compared to the GNN-based flow model is</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Divergence Calculation Speed-up ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The sampling procedure involves a forward pass and one divergence calculation for each integration step. We start by estimating the runtimes of the forward pass <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. The key assumption is that the computational complexity of the forward pass is proportional to the number of edges of the graph (see <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.1 Graph Connectivity ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and the number of message passing steps.:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{f}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{f}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{f}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}\#E^{lg}_{k})=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}nk^{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{f}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}\#E^{lg}_{k})=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}nk^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The computational complexity of the divergence calculation <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT^{\nabla}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∇</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT^{\nabla}</annotation></semantics></math> will be proportional to the number of backward passes necessary to compute the divergence times the complexity for each backward pass. For the fully connected GNN we need <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dn</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes at the cost of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc})</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{\nabla}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc}dn)=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{\nabla}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(T\#E_{fc}dn)=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">For HollowFlow we only need <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> backward passes by exploiting the structure of the Jacobian (See <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Block-hollowness of HoMP). ‣ Euclidean Symmetries of Node Features: ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Each of these backward passes has a complexity <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\#E_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\#E_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, as we only need to backpropagate through the transformer, not the conditioner:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(\#E_{k}d)=\mathcal{O}(dnk)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(\#E_{k}d)=\mathcal{O}(dnk).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">The runtime of one integration step, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{step}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{step}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is proportional to the total runtime, will scale as <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RT}^{f}+\text{RT}^{\nabla}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RT}^{f}+\text{RT}^{\nabla}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we get</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{RT}^{f}(G_{fc})+\text{RT}^{\nabla}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{2})+\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{RT}^{f}(G_{fc})+\text{RT}^{\nabla}(G_{fc})=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{2})+\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d)=\mathcal{O}(Tn^{3}d),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{RT}^{f}(L(G_{k}))+\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}nk^{2})+\mathcal{O}(dnk)=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mi>f</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{RT}^{f}(L(G_{k}))+\text{RT}^{\nabla}(L(G_{k}))=\mathcal{O}(T^{lg}nk^{2})+\mathcal{O}(dnk)=\mathcal{O}(n(T^{lg}k^{2}+dk)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">where the right term in the sum in <a href="#A2.E39" title="In Proof. ‣ B.2 Computational Complexities ‣ Appendix B Derivations and Proofs ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a> dominates as <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{3})&gt;\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{3})&gt;\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant independent of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
The speed-up during sampling with likelihood evaluation when using HollowFlow compared to a standard, fully connected GNN is thus</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right)=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tnd}{T^{lg}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>RT</mtext><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\text{RT}^{step}(G_{fc})}{\text{RT}^{step}(L(G_{k}))}=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tn^{2}d}{T^{lg}k^{2}+dk}\right)=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{Tnd}{T^{lg}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the last equality assumes <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Graph Connectivity ‣ 4 Computational Complexity ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). In conclusion, we expect a speed-up of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for constant <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and a speed-up of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Memory scaling of <math id="A2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the definition of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Permutation Equivariant Conditioner ‣ 3 Hollow Message Passing ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) one might expect a cubic memory scaling in <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it is sufficient to only save information about from which nodes of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> all the nodes of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> have received information from. If <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{lg}</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, the size of the array will effectively be <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nn_{lg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nn_{lg}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{lg}=\mathcal{O}(nk)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{lg}=\mathcal{O}(nk)</annotation></semantics></math> so the memory requirements of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in our implementation scale as <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Results</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Non-equivariant HollowFlow</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adaptation of HollowFlow to non-equivariant GNN architectures is straightforward as HollowFlow only affects the construction of the underlying graph while preserving the message and update functions of the GNN including their equivariance properties. Relaxing permutation symmetry can be done by using unique embeddings for all nodes in the graph (as done for Alanine Dipeptide, see <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Alanine Dipeptide ‣ Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Multi-head HollowFlow</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using a <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN graph poses a locality assumption that might limit the models ability when learning the distribution of systems with long-range interactions (e.g., systems with coulomb interactions such as molecules). To circumvent the locality assumption while still keeping the computational cost manageable, we additional consider a multi-head strategy with <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads. The central idea is to run HoMP on <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> different graphs in parallel and sum up the result. These graphs can be constructed in different ways, we explore the following two strategies:</p>
<ol id="A3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-overlapping scale separation</span>: Consider a fully connected graph <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> embedded in euclidean space with <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E</annotation></semantics></math> edges and sort the edges by length. Divide this list into <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> equally sized chunks of length <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E/H</annotation></semantics></math> with no overlap. If there is a remainder, distribute the remaining edges to the heads as equally as possible.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overlapping scale separation with overlap number <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>: Consider a fully connected graph <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> embedded in euclidean space with <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E</annotation></semantics></math> edges and sort the edges by length. Divide this list into <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> equally sized chunks of size <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E/(H-I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E/(H-I)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the centers of these chunks are spaced evenly. If there is a remainder, distribute the remaining edges to the heads as equally as possible.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Both strategies ensure that there is no locality assumption. The first strategy assumes scale separability while the second one allows for a (limited) scale overlap. In terms of scaling, observe that <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#E=\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#E=\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for a fully connected graph. Thus, each head has <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{2}/(H-I))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{2}/(H-I))</annotation></semantics></math> edges giving us on average <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n/(H-I))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n/(H-I))</annotation></semantics></math> edges per node. Finally, the line graph of each head has thus <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{3}/(H-I)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{3}/(H-I)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> edges, giving us <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Hn^{3}/(H-I)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Hn^{3}/(H-I)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> line graph edges in total. Thus, if we, e.g., choose the number of heads <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the overlap number <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> proportional to <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the scaling is <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We test both of the aforementioned multi-head strategies on LJ13, LJ55 and Alanine Dipeptide. The results together with the single-head results from the main paper can be found in <a href="#A3.T4" title="In C.2 Multi-head HollowFlow ‣ Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ C.2 Multi-head HollowFlow ‣ Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.3 Alanine Dipeptide ‣ Appendix C Additional Results ‣ HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
